  "solution": {
    "approach": "Convert an integer ASCII code to its character representation.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(1)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\"%d\", &n) == 1) {\n        printf(\"%c\\n\", (char)n);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "C++": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    if (cin >> n) {\n        cout << (char)n << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "Java": "import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        if (sc.hasNextInt()) {\n            System.out.println((char)sc.nextInt());\n        }\n    }\n}\n",
      "Python3": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    s = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    if s:\n        print(chr(int(s)))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n",
      "JavaScript": "const fs = require('fs');\n\nfunction main() {\n    const s = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf-8').trim();\n    if (s.length > 0) {\n        console.log(String.fromCharCode(parseInt(s, 10)));\n    }\n}\n\nmain();\n"
    },
    "notes": "Ensure edge case and standard I/O format handling.",
    "editorial": "Cast the input integer to a char (e.g. 65 -> 'A')."
  },
  "hints": [
    "Every character has an underlying integer ASCII code.",
    "In C/Java/C++, cast the integer to (char).",
    "In Python, use chr(val). In JS, use String.fromCharCode(val)."
  ],
